# Little Adventure — Celebration & Progression

Pair with [little-adventure-audio.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-audio.mdc), [child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc), [sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc), [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc), [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc), [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc) (world layers must not break celebration hierarchy), and [architecture.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc).

## Core Principle

Little Adventure should make the child an active participant, not a passive viewer.

After a child successfully completes an activity:

SUCCESS
→ CELEBRATE
→ ACKNOWLEDGE
→ GUIDE
→ CHILD ACTS
→ CONTINUE

The application must not automatically move the child forward after a normal challenge.

The child should understand:
"I did something → I got positive feedback → now I need to do something."

---

## 1. Celebration

A successful challenge should have a short, positive celebration.

The celebration may include:
- Character animation
- Gentle visual effects
- Small stars/bubbles
- Short cheerful jingle
- Positive voice feedback

Keep celebrations:
- Warm
- Playful
- Short
- Predictable
- Sensory-friendly

Avoid:
- Flashing
- Rapid movement
- Loud sounds
- Excessive confetti
- Game-show style effects
- Long animations that delay interaction

The visible success message for normal challenges is:

"Good Job!"

Do not use "Hooray!" or "Hoorah!" as the primary success message.

The existing centralized audio system should provide the corresponding "Good Job!" voice.

Visual treatment of "Good Job!" is defined in **Good Job Visual Treatment** below.

---

## 1A. Celebration Composition

The celebration must be treated as ONE coordinated visual scene/state.

All celebration elements belong to the same composition:
- Good Job message
- existing mascot
- balloons
- bubbles
- small stars/sprinkles
- celebration motion
- celebration audio

These elements must not appear as separate sequential UI screens.

When the celebration begins, the child should immediately see a coherent celebration scene.

Do NOT create an intermediate visible state where:
- Good Job appears before the other celebration elements
- balloons/bubbles appear several seconds later
- the mascot appears after the celebration has already started
- Next is visible while celebration elements are still entering
- the celebration looks incomplete while other elements are loading/animating

Small individual animations are allowed, but they must be coordinated.
The child should perceive them as one celebration, not a sequence of separate screens.

The celebration should have one clear visual hierarchy:

1. Good Job
2. Celebrating mascot
3. Supporting decorative effects
4. No Next action yet

The Next action belongs to the GUIDANCE state, not the CELEBRATION state.

---

## Good Job Visual Treatment

"Good Job!" should feel like part of the Little Adventure game world.

It must NOT look like:
- a notification
- a toast
- a dashboard card
- a generic shadcn/ui component
- a web-app alert
- a heavy rectangular UI panel

The Good Job treatment should feel playful, warm, illustrated, and integrated with the existing Little Adventure scene.

The existing mascot and celebration environment should visually relate to the Good Job message.

Prioritize visual integration over adding more decoration.

---

## 2. No Automatic Progression

For normal challenges, NEVER automatically advance to the next challenge because:

- A timer completed
- A celebration animation completed
- A voice clip completed
- A sound effect completed
- A motion animation completed

After the celebration finishes, the application must wait for an explicit child action.

This is an intentional interaction boundary.

---

## 3. Guided Next Action

After the celebration finishes, present a clear next action.

The child should receive BOTH:

1. A visual cue
2. A spoken instruction

The visual cue should clearly identify the interactive target.

Preferred pattern:

        👉
     NEXT →

The pointer/arrow should point toward the actual Next button.

The visual cue should be:
- Large enough to notice
- Friendly
- Simple
- Gently animated
- Easy to understand
- Tablet-friendly

Use subtle bounce/pulse motion.

Do not use:
- Flashing arrows
- Rapid movement
- Excessive animation
- Multiple competing visual targets

---

## 4. Spoken Guidance

When the Next action becomes available, the child should hear:

"Click here to proceed."

This is an interaction instruction.

Use the centralized Little Adventure audio architecture.

Do not use:
- Browser speech synthesis
- `new Audio(...)`
- Ad-hoc HTML audio
- Separate audio state
- Game-specific audio managers

The instruction should work with the existing music ducking behavior.

If the voice asset does not exist yet, do not invent or generate one in code.

Register the required semantic asset and clearly report the missing asset.

---

## 5. Synchronization

The visual and audio instruction should begin together.

When:

"Click here to proceed."

plays:

- Next button is visible
- Pointer is visible
- Pointer points toward Next
- Button has a subtle visual affordance

The child should not have to read text to understand the instruction.

---

## 6. Next Button

The Next button should:

- Be large
- Have a large touch target
- Be visually obvious
- Match Little Adventure's existing visual language
- Be easy for a young child to tap

Do not introduce unnecessary UI complexity.

Reuse existing components/styles where appropriate.

The button should not be active before the celebration reaches the appropriate point.

---

## 7. Child Action

When the child taps Next:

1. End the guidance state.
2. Stop/hide the pointer.
3. Play the existing appropriate transition/interaction sound.
4. Move to the next challenge.
5. Start the next challenge normally.
6. Allow the next challenge's instruction system to work normally.

Background music must NOT restart.

---

## 8. Final Challenge

The final challenge is different from normal challenge progression.

Normal challenge:

SUCCESS
→ Celebration
→ Good Job!
→ Click here to proceed
→ Child taps Next
→ Next challenge

Final challenge:

SUCCESS
→ Final celebration
→ Final success feedback
→ Existing completion flow

Do not force the normal Next pattern onto the final completion flow unless explicitly requested.

The final completion experience should remain a distinct experience.

---

## 9. Consistency Across Games

All existing Little Adventure games should use the same celebration-to-progression principle.

A child should not have to learn a different interaction pattern for every game.

Normal challenge progression should consistently be:

Celebrate → Guide → Child taps → Continue.

---

## 10. Audio Architecture

Use the centralized audio system.

Semantic audio concepts include:

- interaction
- final interaction
- success
- retry
- instruction
- transition
- final success

Do not create duplicate audio systems.

Voice should remain separate from music and effects.

Music should duck appropriately during voice instructions.

Audio OFF must continue to suppress audio while preserving the visual interaction cue.

The experience must remain understandable visually when audio is disabled.

---

## 11. Accessibility / Sensory Design

Little Adventure is designed for young children.

Prefer:
- Gentle motion
- Clear visual hierarchy
- Large targets
- Predictable interactions
- Positive reinforcement
- Low cognitive load

Avoid:
- Flashing
- Sudden movement
- Sudden loud audio
- Negative/error sounds
- "Game Over"
- Lost lives/hearts
- Harsh failure states

---

## 12. Implementation Philosophy

Do not over-engineer.

Prefer:
- Small reusable components
- Existing Little Adventure components
- Existing audio architecture
- Existing state management patterns

Do not create a generic game engine solely to implement celebration behavior.

Do not redesign unrelated screens.

Do not add parent settings unless explicitly requested.

Do not add future game functionality unless explicitly requested.

---

## 13. Validation

Any implementation involving celebration/progression must verify:

- No unintended automatic progression
- Celebration plays once
- Celebration appears as one coordinated scene (not sequential screens)
- Good Job is integrated with the scene (not a toast/card/alert)
- Good Job voice plays once
- Next is not visible during the celebration state
- Next instruction plays at the correct time
- Pointer appears at the correct time
- Next button requires explicit child interaction
- Pointer disappears after Next
- Next challenge starts correctly
- Background music does not restart
- Existing audio ducking still works
- Audio OFF still preserves visual guidance
